---
/**
 * universal-senior-benefits-screener.astro
 * Category: Senior Financial Assistance & Benefits Screeners
 * Primary Keyword: senior benefits screener
 * Supporting Keywords: what benefits do seniors qualify for, senior assistance programs 2026,
 *   snap liheap medicare savings program eligibility, benefits for low income seniors
 */
import ToolLayout from '@layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import { UniversalBenefitsScreener } from '@components/tools/UniversalBenefitsScreener';
import NoSignupStatement from '@components/common/NoSignupStatement.astro';
import DisclaimerBlock from '@components/content/DisclaimerBlock.astro';
import FAQBlock from '@components/content/FAQBlock.astro';

const lastReviewed = 'July 2026';

const faqs = [
  { question: 'What benefits programs does this screener check?', answer: 'This screener evaluates your eligibility for SNAP (food assistance), LIHEAP (energy help), Medicare Savings Programs (Part B premium help), Extra Help (Part D prescription costs), SSI (income support), Lifeline (phone/internet subsidy), and flags potential property tax relief based on your state.' },
  { question: 'How accurate is this screener compared to applying officially?', answer: 'We use the official 2026 federal income and asset guidelines to provide a highly accurate estimate of your eligibility. However, only the specific government agency can make a final determination. We recommend applying if this screener indicates you are likely eligible.' },
  { question: 'Does using this screener affect my eligibility or my benefits?', answer: 'No. This is a completely private, anonymous educational tool. We do not ask for your name, Social Security number, or contact information, and we do not share your results with any government agency.' },
  { question: 'What is the 2026 federal poverty level for a single senior?', answer: 'For 2026, the 100% federal poverty level (FPL) for a single individual in the contiguous United States is $1,255 per month. Many senior assistance programs use multipliers of this number, such as 135% or 150%, to determine eligibility.' },
  { question: 'Can I qualify for more than one of these programs at the same time?', answer: 'Yes. In fact, qualifying for one program often makes it easier to qualify for others. For example, if you qualify for SSI, you often automatically qualify for Medicaid and Extra Help. Many seniors receive SNAP, LIHEAP, and Medicare Savings Programs simultaneously.' },
  { question: 'My income is slightly above the limits — am I definitely disqualified?', answer: 'Not necessarily. Many programs allow deductions that lower your "countable" income. For instance, SNAP allows seniors to deduct out-of-pocket medical expenses, which can bring a borderline income down to a qualifying level.' },
  { question: 'What is the Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP) and do seniors get special rules?', answer: 'SNAP provides monthly funds on an EBT card to buy groceries. Yes, households with a member 60 or older or with a disability use special rules: they do not have to pass the gross income test, only the net income test, and can deduct medical expenses.' },
  { question: 'What is a Medicare Savings Program and how much can it save me?', answer: 'Medicare Savings Programs are state-run programs that help pay your Medicare Part B premiums, and sometimes Part A premiums and cost-sharing. The most common benefit pays the standard Part B premium, which saves you over $2,000 per year.' },
];
---

<ToolLayout
  title="Universal Senior Benefits Screener — Free 2026 | Seniors Audit"
  description="Free senior benefits screener — check SNAP, LIHEAP, Medicare Savings Programs, Extra Help, and SSI eligibility in 3 minutes. No account needed."
  faqs={faqs}
  schema="HowTo"
  howToSteps={[
    { name: "Step 1 — Enter household size, age, and state", text: "Select how many people live in your household, whether anyone is 60+ or disabled, and your state. State determines LIHEAP limits and property tax programs." },
    { name: "Step 2 — Enter monthly gross income and asset totals", text: "Enter combined gross monthly income and countable assets. The screener applies the correct exclusions for each program automatically." },
    { name: "Step 3 — Review eligibility results for all programs", text: "See a color-coded eligibility estimate for each of the 7 programs, with the specific threshold and your margin above or below it." },
    { name: "Step 4 — Follow the direct apply links for programs you qualify for", text: "Each program result includes a direct link to the official application portal. Apply for all qualifying programs — benefits stack and don't cancel each other out." }
  ]}
>

  <!-- ELEMENT 2 — HERO -->
  <div class="ubs-hero">
    <div class="ubs-breadcrumb">
      <a href="/tools/" class="ubs-breadcrumb-link">Tools &amp; Calculators</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true"> › </span>
      <span>Universal Senior Benefits Screener</span>
    </div>

    <h1 class="ubs-h1">
      Universal Senior Benefits Screener — Check SNAP, LIHEAP, Medicare Savings Programs, Extra Help &amp; More in One Pass
    </h1>
    <p class="ubs-date">Figures last verified against official federal sources in {lastReviewed}.</p>

    <div class="ubs-aeo-block" role="note" aria-label="Quick Answer">
      <p class="ubs-aeo-label">Quick Answer</p>
      <p class="ubs-aeo-text">
        A single senior with income around <strong class="ubs-gold">$1,100/month</strong> may qualify for <strong class="ubs-gold">5 or more programs simultaneously</strong> — SNAP food assistance, LIHEAP energy help, QMB (pays Medicare Part A+B premiums and cost-sharing), <a href="/tools/extra-help-eligibility-calculator/" class="ubs-aeo-link">Extra Help</a> (eliminates Part D drug costs), and SSI. Combined annual benefit value can exceed <strong class="ubs-gold">$9,000–$15,000/year</strong>. These programs don't cancel each other out — they stack.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p class="ubs-problem">
      Most seniors apply for programs one at a time — sometimes waiting months between applications — and never realize they qualified for several simultaneously. A single 15-minute screener covering all major programs is something no government website provides. This tool checks 7 federal and state assistance programs against your income and household in one pass, surfacing every program worth applying for before you make a single phone call or visit a single office.
    </p>

    <ul class="ubs-who-list" aria-label="Who this tool is for">
      <li class="ubs-who-item"><span class="ubs-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span> Seniors on Social Security income below $1,500/month who have never screened for the full range of federal assistance programs they may already qualify for.</li>
      <li class="ubs-who-item"><span class="ubs-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span> Newly enrolled Medicare beneficiaries who want to check whether a <a href="/tools/medicare-savings-estimator/" class="ubs-inline-link">Medicare Savings Program</a> can pay their Part B premium before their first premium bill arrives.</li>
      <li class="ubs-who-item"><span class="ubs-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span> Caregivers helping an aging parent identify all available financial assistance programs before a costly care transition — when every dollar of savings matters most.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <!-- ELEMENT 3 — TOOL -->
  <div class="ubs-tool-block" id="screener">
    <UniversalBenefitsScreener client:load />
  </div>

  <NoSignupStatement />

  <!-- ELEMENT 4 — HOW TO USE -->
  <section class="ubs-section" aria-labelledby="how-to-heading">
    <h2 id="how-to-heading">How to Use the Senior Benefits Screener — Step by Step</h2>
    <ol class="ubs-steps">
      <li><strong>Step 1 — Enter household size, ages, and state.</strong> Select how many people live in your household. Indicate whether anyone is 60 or older or has a disability — this triggers the special elderly household rules for SNAP (no gross income test, medical deductions allowed). Your state determines which LIHEAP income limits apply and which property tax programs are available.</li>
      <li><strong>Step 2 — Enter your total monthly gross income.</strong> Include all sources: Social Security (full amount before Medicare deductions), pension payments, wages, interest, dividends, and rental income. The screener applies the correct income-counting rules and deductions for each program separately — the same income is evaluated differently by SNAP, MSP, and Extra Help.</li>
      <li><strong>Step 3 — Enter countable assets.</strong> Enter cash, bank accounts, stocks, bonds, and retirement account values. Do not count your primary home, one vehicle, or personal belongings — most federal assistance programs exclude these. The screener notes which programs have asset tests and which don't.</li>
      <li><strong>Step 4 — Review all results and apply for qualifying programs.</strong> Each program result includes the specific threshold, your margin, and a direct link to the official application. Apply for <em>all</em> programs flagged as likely qualifying. Benefits from different programs don't reduce each other — receiving <a href="/tools/liheap-eligibility-checker/" class="ubs-inline-link">LIHEAP energy assistance</a> does not reduce your SNAP benefits.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="ubs-pro-tip">
      <p class="ubs-tip-label">💡 Pro Tip</p>
      <p class="ubs-tip-text">If the screener shows you're over the income limit for a program like SNAP, don't stop there. SNAP allows seniors to deduct out-of-pocket medical costs above $35/month from their countable income. Run the detailed <a href="/tools/snap-eligibility-calculator-for-seniors/" class="ubs-tip-link">SNAP Eligibility Calculator for Seniors</a> separately to apply the medical expense deduction — it frequently changes a "not eligible" result to a qualifying one for borderline-income seniors.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Programs Overview Table -->
    <div class="ubs-table-section">
      <h3>Programs Covered — 2026 Income Thresholds at a Glance</h3>
      <div class="ubs-table-wrap">
        <table class="ubs-ref-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Program</th><th>What It Covers</th><th>Single Income Limit (approx.)</th><th>Asset Test?</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>SNAP</strong></td><td>Monthly grocery funds via EBT card</td><td>$1,255/mo net (no gross test for 60+)</td><td>No for 60+</td></tr>
            <tr class="ubs-row-alt"><td><strong>LIHEAP</strong></td><td>Heating/cooling utility bills</td><td>~$1,883/mo (150% FPL; states vary)</td><td>No</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>QMB (MSP)</strong></td><td>Part A+B premiums + cost-sharing</td><td>~$1,255/mo</td><td>$9,090</td></tr>
            <tr class="ubs-row-alt"><td><strong>SLMB (MSP)</strong></td><td>Part B premium only ($185/mo)</td><td>~$1,504/mo</td><td>$9,090</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Extra Help (LIS)</strong></td><td>Part D drug premium + copays</td><td>~$1,985/mo</td><td>$17,220</td></tr>
            <tr class="ubs-row-alt"><td><strong>SSI</strong></td><td>Monthly income supplement</td><td>$967/mo (2026 federal benefit rate)</td><td>$2,000</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Lifeline</strong></td><td>Phone/internet bill discount ($30.25/mo)</td><td>135% FPL (~$1,695/mo)</td><td>No</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="ubs-table-note">Source: SSA, USDA FNS, HHS ACF, and FCC official 2026 program guidelines. Limits shown are for a single individual in the contiguous United States.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ELEMENT 5 — SCENARIOS -->
  <section class="ubs-section" aria-labelledby="scenarios-heading">
    <h2 id="scenarios-heading">Real-Life Screening Examples — What the Results Look Like</h2>

    <div class="ubs-scenario-card">
      <div class="ubs-scenario-header">
        <span class="ubs-tag">Scenario 1</span>
        <strong class="ubs-scenario-name">Gloria, Age 72, Single — Low Income, Qualifies for 5 Programs</strong>
      </div>
      <p class="ubs-scenario-desc">Gloria receives $1,100/month in Social Security and has $4,000 in a savings account. Her home is owned free-and-clear but excluded. She has never applied for any assistance programs.</p>
      <table class="ubs-scenario-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Program</th><th>Result</th><th>Estimated Annual Value</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>SNAP</td><td class="ubs-yes">✅ Qualifies</td><td>~$1,800/year ($150/mo)</td></tr>
          <tr class="ubs-row-alt"><td>LIHEAP</td><td class="ubs-yes">✅ Qualifies</td><td>~$700/season</td></tr>
          <tr><td>QMB (Medicare Savings Program)</td><td class="ubs-yes">✅ Qualifies — pays Part A+B premiums</td><td>~$2,220+/year</td></tr>
          <tr class="ubs-row-alt"><td>Extra Help (LIS)</td><td class="ubs-yes">✅ Auto-qualifies via QMB</td><td>~$5,900/year</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SSI</td><td class="ubs-maybe">⚠️ Possible — income near $967 limit</td><td>Evaluate separately</td></tr>
          <tr class="ubs-row-alt"><td><strong>Total estimated annual value</strong></td><td colspan="2"><strong>$9,800+/year</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="ubs-result-block">
        <span class="ubs-result-highlight">✅ $9,800+/Year in Benefits Found — From $0 in Current Enrollments</span>
        Gloria had been paying her full Part B premium ($185/month), her Part D premium ($42/month), and full drug copays — a total of ~$3,624/year she no longer needs to pay. QMB auto-qualifies her for Extra Help, eliminating drug costs entirely.
      </div>
      <p class="ubs-action"><strong>Key:</strong> Gloria's QMB qualification auto-triggers Extra Help (LIS) status — she doesn't need to apply for Extra Help separately. Learn more about how QMB and Extra Help interact at our <a href="/tools/medicare-savings-estimator/" class="ubs-inline-link">Medicare Savings Programs Estimator</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="ubs-scenario-card">
      <div class="ubs-scenario-header">
        <span class="ubs-tag">Scenario 2</span>
        <strong class="ubs-scenario-name">Harold &amp; Betty, Ages 68 &amp; 65 — Moderate Income, 3 Programs</strong>
      </div>
      <p class="ubs-scenario-desc">Harold and Betty receive combined Social Security of $2,050/month and have $22,000 in savings. Harold has Medicare Part A and B. They thought their income was too high for any assistance.</p>
      <table class="ubs-scenario-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Program</th><th>Result</th><th>Annual Value</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>SNAP</td><td class="ubs-no">❌ Over net income limit (couple)</td><td>$0</td></tr>
          <tr class="ubs-row-alt"><td>LIHEAP</td><td class="ubs-yes">✅ Qualifies (state limits vary)</td><td>~$600–$900/season</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SLMB (MSP — pays Part B premium)</td><td class="ubs-yes">✅ Qualifies</td><td>$2,220/year ($185/mo)</td></tr>
          <tr class="ubs-row-alt"><td>Extra Help (LIS)</td><td class="ubs-yes">✅ SLMB auto-qualifies</td><td>~$1,200/year partial</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SSI</td><td class="ubs-no">❌ Over income limit</td><td>$0</td></tr>
          <tr class="ubs-row-alt"><td><strong>Total estimated annual value</strong></td><td colspan="2"><strong>~$3,800–$4,300/year</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="ubs-result-block">
        <span class="ubs-result-highlight">✅ $3,800/Year Found — Despite Income That "Felt Too High" for Benefits</span>
        Harold and Betty assumed their $2,050/month income disqualified them from everything. The SLMB alone saves $185/month on Harold's Part B premium. SLMB auto-triggers LIS/Extra Help status, reducing their Part D costs significantly.
      </div>
      <p class="ubs-action"><strong>Key:</strong> SLMB income limits for couples are significantly higher than most seniors realize — ~$2,720/month in many states for 2026. Apply through your state Medicaid office, not SSA. Check exact limits using our <a href="/tools/medicare-savings-estimator/" class="ubs-inline-link">Medicare Savings Programs Estimator</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="ubs-scenario-card">
      <div class="ubs-scenario-header">
        <span class="ubs-tag">Scenario 3</span>
        <strong class="ubs-scenario-name">Patricia, Age 77, Single — Higher Income, Niche Programs Still Found</strong>
      </div>
      <p class="ubs-scenario-desc">Patricia receives $1,900/month in SS + pension income and has $45,000 in savings. She doesn't qualify for most income-tested programs — but the screener flags two specific programs she qualifies for regardless.</p>
      <table class="ubs-scenario-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Program</th><th>Result</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>SNAP, SSI, QMB, SLMB, Extra Help</td><td class="ubs-no">❌ Over income or asset limits</td><td>$0</td></tr>
          <tr class="ubs-row-alt"><td>Lifeline phone/internet subsidy</td><td class="ubs-yes">✅ Qualifies (income at 135% FPL)</td><td>$363/year ($30.25/mo)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Property tax relief (state-specific)</td><td class="ubs-yes">✅ Flagged — check state exemption</td><td>$400–$1,800/year</td></tr>
          <tr class="ubs-row-alt"><td>IRMAA appeal eligibility</td><td class="ubs-maybe">⚠️ Flagged — income drop possible</td><td>Potentially $1,000+/year</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="ubs-result-block">
        <span class="ubs-result-highlight">✅ $800–$2,200/Year Found Even at Higher Income — Two Programs Others Miss</span>
        Patricia's Lifeline savings ($363/year) and property tax exemption (varies by state) are available regardless of her higher income. The screener also flags an IRMAA appeal — if her pension income recently dropped, she may qualify to reduce Medicare surcharges immediately using our <a href="/tools/should-i-appeal-my-irmaa/" class="ubs-inline-link">IRMAA Appeal Wizard</a>.
      </div>
      <p class="ubs-action"><strong>Key:</strong> No income level is too high to screen. Lifeline, property tax programs, and IRMAA appeals often apply to seniors the main income-tested programs miss. Use our <a href="/tools/property-tax-relief-checker/" class="ubs-inline-link">Property Tax Relief Checker</a> for her specific state exemption details.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="ubs-disclaimer">These are representative examples using official 2026 federal program thresholds. Individual eligibility depends on your specific state rules, household composition, and deductions. Apply through official government portals for a final determination.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ELEMENT 6 — PITFALLS -->
  <section class="ubs-section" aria-labelledby="pitfalls-heading">
    <h2 id="pitfalls-heading">Common Benefits Screening Mistakes — And How to Avoid Them</h2>

    <h3>Applying for One Program at a Time Instead of Screening All at Once</h3>
    <p>Most seniors apply for SNAP first, wait for a determination, then consider LIHEAP, then look into Medicare Savings Programs. This sequential approach takes months and often results in missing programs entirely. Benefits stack — SNAP doesn't reduce LIHEAP, and LIHEAP doesn't reduce Extra Help. All programs can be applied for simultaneously.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to screen all programs in one pass (which this tool does), then apply for every qualifying program in the same week. Application documents largely overlap — your Social Security award letter, Medicare card, and most recent tax return cover most applications. Find all application portals at <a href="https://www.benefits.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">benefits.gov</a>.</p>

    <h3>Assuming Assets Automatically Disqualify You From Multiple Programs</h3>
    <p>Each program has its own asset rules — and some have none at all. SNAP has no asset test for households with a member 60+. LIHEAP has no asset test. Extra Help has a $17,220 single / $34,360 married limit — but excludes your home, car, and personal belongings. SSI has a $2,000 limit, but again excludes home and one vehicle. Many seniors with $30,000–$60,000 in savings qualify for several programs.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to let the screener calculate asset eligibility for each program separately, using the correct exclusions. Never self-disqualify based on a total savings number before accounting for excluded asset categories. See SSA's asset exclusion rules at <a href="https://www.ssa.gov/medicare/savings-programs" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SSA Medicare Savings Programs</a>.</p>

    <h3>Not Re-Applying at Annual Renewal Time</h3>
    <p>Extra Help, Medicare Savings Programs, and LIHEAP are all subject to annual income verification and renewal. SSA sends a yellow Extra Help re-verification letter each fall. States mail MSP renewal notices annually. LIHEAP applications open and close by season. Many seniors who were enrolled lose benefits simply by not responding to renewal notices — and then struggle to re-enroll for another year.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to set a calendar reminder for October each year to: (1) check for SSA's yellow Extra Help letter, (2) respond to any state MSP renewal notice, and (3) check your state's LIHEAP application window opening date. Re-enrollment is faster than initial enrollment — most take under 10 minutes.</p>

    <h3>Stopping After the First Program Denial Without Checking Others</h3>
    <p>A denial from SNAP does not mean LIHEAP will deny you. A denial from Extra Help does not mean you can't get an <a href="/tools/medicare-savings-estimator/" class="ubs-inline-link">MSP that pays your Part B premium</a>. Each program has different income definitions, deduction rules, and thresholds. Seniors who stop searching after one denial frequently leave thousands of dollars in annual benefits unclaimed.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to treat each program as independent and run all of them through this screener regardless of prior denials. A SNAP denial based on gross income doesn't account for the medical expense deduction — use our standalone <a href="/tools/snap-eligibility-calculator-for-seniors/" class="ubs-inline-link">SNAP Eligibility Calculator for Seniors</a> to recalculate with the medical deduction before accepting a denial as final.</p>
  </section>

